Question: "why is the 10 percent attribution coefficient from Spalding et al. 2017 used" (Context: Lima, Tab: diagnostics)
Response (2.28s) | Title: Economic Attribution Methodology | Pose: answer | Links: 2
  [CONCEPT]: The 10 percent attribution coefficient from Spalding et al. (2017) is utilized to isolate the portion of visitor expenditure directly attributable to the presence of healthy coral reefs. This conservative proxy prevents the overestimation of reef-dependent tourism revenue by filtering out general travel spending (e.g., transport, accommodation) that would occur regardless of the reef's ecological condition.
  [FORMULA]: The Reef-Adjacent Economy is calculated as: 1,464,770 annual visitors * RM410 average expenditure * 0.10 attribution factor = RM60.09M/year. This provides a standardized, defensible baseline for assessing the economic impact of reef health across 30 monitoring units.
  [DATA SOURCE]: This methodology integrates the DOSM Domestic Tourism Survey 2024 (RM410 average spend) with the global reef-dependency framework established by Spalding et al. (2017) to ensure institutional consistency with OpenDOSM data standards.
  [LINKS]: DOSM Domestic Tourism Survey 2024, OpenDOSM Data Catalogue

