=== [Iter 0123] 2026-09-16 13:11:18 | Category: Policy & Econ ===
Question: "what should a marine park ranger do when an island is in high screening priority" (Context: Lang Tengah, Tab: evidence)
Response (2.68s) | Title: Operational Protocol for High Screening Priority Units | Pose: warn | Links: 3
  [PRIORITY DEFINITION]: High screening priority indicates a monitoring unit has been flagged by the ReefSafe Gradient Boosting Regressor for urgent field verification due to a significant negative predicted change in live coral cover. This is a biological monitoring metric, not a human safety risk assessment.
  [RANGER ACTION PLAN]: 1. Conduct immediate in-situ 100m transect surveys to validate the RCM-derived model inputs.
2. Inspect for localized disturbance indicators such as coral bleaching, crown-of-thorns starfish outbreaks, or physical damage.
3. Review local water quality indicators (BOD, ammoniacal nitrogen) from the nearest river basin monitoring stations.
4. Report findings to the Department of Fisheries Malaysia (DOFM) Marine Parks Section for potential management intervention.
  [DATA SOURCES]: Rangers should cross-reference field observations with the Reef Check Malaysia annual survey reports and the OpenDOSM water quality datasets to identify potential drivers of the observed decline.
  [LINKS]: Reef Check Malaysia annual reports, OpenDOSM Data Catalogue, DOFM Marine Parks
